==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wert-}}
First attested around 1374, from {{der|en|fro|avers}} (French {{m|fr|adverse}}), from {{der|en|la|adversus||turned against}}, past participle of {{m|la|advertere}}, from {{m|la|ad-||to}} + {{m|la|vertere||to turn}}. See also {{m|en|versus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæd.və(ɹ)s/|/ədˈvɜ(ɹ)s/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-adverse.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜ(ɹ)s|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|more,er<l:rare,or,nonstandard>}}

# [[unfavorable|Unfavorable]]; [[antagonistic]] in purpose or effect; [[hostile]]; actively opposing one's interests or wishes; contrary to one's welfare; acting against; working in an opposing direction.
#: {{coi|en|'''adverse''' criticism}}
#: {{coi|en|'''adverse''' weather}}
#* {{RQ:Southey Thomas More|passage=Happy were it for us all if we bore prosperity as well and wisely as we endure an '''adverse''' fortune.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=December 14, 2011
|author=Steven Morris
|title=Devon woman jailed for 168 days for killing kitten in microwave
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/14/woman-killed-kitten-microwave-jail
|passage=He said Robins had not been in trouble with the law before and had no previous convictions. Jail would have an '''adverse''' effect on her and her three children as she was the main carer.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013161452/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/dec/14/woman-killed-kitten-microwave-jail|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# [[opposed|Opposed]]; [[contrary]]; opposing one's interests or desire.
#: {{co|en|'''adverse''' circumstances}}
# {{lb|en|not comparable}} [[opposite|Opposite]]; [[confronting]].
#: {{co|en|the '''adverse''' page}}
#: {{co|en|the '''adverse''' party}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1809|author=[[w:George Gordon Byron, 6th Baron Byron|Lord Byron]]|title=w:English Bards and Scotch Reviewers|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=3agTAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA272&dq=adverse|passage=Calpe's '''adverse''' height / {{...}} must greet my sight}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1835|author=James Hogg|title=The Story of Euphemia Hewit|passage=Now the families of the two lovers were not on very good terms; they were, I believe, rather '''adverse''' to one another.}}

====Usage notes====
''Adverse'' is sometimes confused with {{m|en|averse}}, though the meanings are somewhat different. ''Adverse'' most often refers to things, denoting something that is in opposition to someone's interests — something one might refer to as an {{m|en|adversity}} or {{m|en|adversary}} — (''adverse winds''; ''an attitude adverse to our ideals''). ''Averse'' usually refers to people, and implies one has a distaste, disinclination, or {{m|en|aversion}} toward something (''a leader averse to war''; ''an investor averse to risk taking''). ''Averse'' is most often used with "''to''" in a construction like "''I am averse to…''". ''Adverse'' shows up less often in this type of construction, describing a person instead of a thing, and should carry a meaning of "actively opposed to" rather than "has an aversion to".

